    + ' ' + 'FROST SWEEP (2026-10-04, after batch 2–3; Owner: “an opaque band under the top bar cuts the glass page in two — and anything like it does not fit the glass”). Walked on a branch dev server at 1440, dark and light, against the frost, ?frost=0 and Reduce transparency, and every static route probed for opaque elements across the glass page. SHELL: the market strip (Open orders · Market Streams · Daily % · Daily $ · symbols) painted bg-card and an --sk-line edge — it sits in the page glass but outside the lane, so neither the lane’s frost inks nor its ink-6% --border reached it. The prototype draws no such band; the top bar above it is clear with no standing edge (_Shell TopBar L144–149). It is now a frost band (data-frost-band, index.css): the lane’s inks (card clear, muted / secondary / accent ink 8%), its edge and divider the DS table rule (ink 6%); skeleton the same; ?frost=0 / solid keep the card and the line. The inspector is the other glass host outside a page-material scope (it portals out of the lane), so a body’s border-border head or divider drew --sk-line across its glass (Vol ratings’ Why head): under the frost its --border is the table rule too. PAGE: Book’s Demand / Supply cells were raised wells over an --sk-line0 grid showing through a 1px gap; under the frost the wells went to ink 4% and the whole grid read as an opaque --sk-line0 block (grey slab on paper). The cells now sit on the panel’s fill with one hairline between them, as the prototype draws them (L22 `.ps-ds > div + div`): beside each other a rule down, stacked a rule across (the grid is the cells’ container, two columns from 2 × 13.125rem). Left as they are, for the Owner: the Symbol list column keeps its solid --sk-raised ground (the prototype re-pins _Part SymbolDock solid; it sits beside the glass page, not on it); this page’s page-wide ink-4% group is a group fill, not a cut.'
    + ' ' + 'REV .155 MARKET STRIP RETIRED (2026-10-04, Package .56, _Shell TopBar ORDERS / ordOf, Shell Spec §4, walked on a branch dev server at 1440, dark and light, orders injected into the status read because DEV had none — a Sunday). SHELL: GlobalMarketStatusBar is gone, with its hook, its route list, its summary util and the frost-band CSS (the inspector keeps the table-rule border); the page glass now starts right under the top bar. Its four readings: working orders → the Account control; Market Streams → the Control Center’s Market data row (it was already there, and it counts in Can I trade while the centre is open — the closed bar’s Data lamp leaves it out on purpose, because judging it holds the quote stream); the subscribed set’s Daily % / Daily $ → deleted; the symbols list → Market › Live, whose Market Streams table already lists the same names and more (subscribed ∪ held ∪ watchlist) with per-row Daily $ / Daily % from the same daily-change function. ACCOUNT CONTROL: a clock and N after the Δ, only while N > 0, in --sk-mute2 (not a warning); the tip says ◷ N working orders at IB. The Book centre’s wide Δ tile split into Δ (third column) and Orders (fourth); Orders opens the list — the contract in --sk-contract, side · IB status · account, LMT price — each row to /trade/fills; empty reads “No working orders at IB.” Both follow the account scope (an order with no account stays under every scope, as the book rows do). Source: the status read the strip used (portfolio.open_orders, brokerage.open_orders in PostgreSQL — the same rows GET /open-orders gives Live and Orders & Fills), already polled by this control, so no new request. DIVERGED, named: the prototype’s “working 14m” has no field behind it — the open-orders snapshot is rewritten whole on every sync with updated_ts = now(), so it is when the row was last seen, not when the order went in; the row shows the IB status where the age would go and the snapshot’s age in its title. A remaining quantity is shown (not the original size), filled-of-total in the title. Same round, the Objective chip: when the objectives read is refused for want of a Research user (401, no token) it reads “Research user not set” (ResearchAuthGap’s wording; — for a refused token or another failure) instead of “No objective”, and its popover offers Set user. Waiting for the Owner’s look.'
